<h1>Reading a Florida Bill's <em>Status</em></h1>
<div class="standfirst">A bill's status line is written for people who already know the rules. Here's what Florida's version actually says — and why most bills end without anyone voting them down.</div>

<h2>The 60-day clock</h2>
<p>Florida's session length isn't a custom or a schedule — it's in the constitution, which is what makes the deadline so unforgiving. Article III, Section 3 sets it out:</p>
<blockquote class="pullq">"A regular session of the legislature shall not exceed sixty consecutive days, and a special session shall not exceed twenty consecutive days, unless extended beyond such limit by a three-fifths vote of each house."</blockquote>
<p>Even an extension doesn't reopen the floor: during one, <em>"no new business may be taken up in either house without the consent of two-thirds of its membership."</em></p>
<p>When the session convenes is fixed the same way — <em>"on the first Tuesday after the first Monday in March of each odd-numbered year, and on the second Tuesday after the first Monday in January of each even-numbered year."</em> Check the <a href="https://www.flsenate.gov/Session" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Senate's session calendar ↗</a> for the current year's exact dates.</p>
<p>The practical consequence: <strong>time is the most common way a Florida bill dies.</strong> Not a vote against it — the clock.</p>

<h2>Life of a bill</h2>
<p>The Legislature's own summary of the path, in its order:</p>
<div class="receipts"><ul>
  <li><span>A legislator sponsors the bill</span><span class="receipt-srcs">it is then referred to one or more committees related to its subject</span></li>
  <li><span>Committee considers it</span><span class="receipt-srcs">the committee may amend it, pass it, or reject it</span></li>
  <li><span>If it passes committee</span><span class="receipt-srcs">it moves to other committees of reference, or to the full house</span></li>
  <li><span>Floor vote</span><span class="receipt-srcs">the full chamber votes</span></li>
  <li><span>The other chamber repeats all of it</span><span class="receipt-srcs">same committee and floor process, from the start</span></li>
  <li><span>Back and forth until both agree</span><span class="receipt-srcs">a bill can move between houses until a consensus is reached</span></li>
</ul></div>
<p>The Legislature adds the part worth remembering: <em>the measure could fail at any point in the process.</em> A bill has to survive every one of those steps. It only has to die once.</p>

<h2>The words Florida uses</h2>
<p>Definitions below are the Senate's own, quoted from its glossary.</p>
<div class="receipts"><ul>
  <li><span>Commit</span><span class="receipt-srcs">"To refer a bill to a committee."</span></li>
  <li><span>Engrossed <b>(E1, 1st Eng.)</b></span><span class="receipt-srcs">"The version of a measure that incorporates adopted floor amendments."</span></li>
  <li><span>Enrolled <b>(ER)</b></span><span class="receipt-srcs">"Approved by both houses and signed by the legislative officers, … sent to the Governor for action."</span></li>
  <li><span>Companion bill</span><span class="receipt-srcs">"A bill introduced in one house that is substantially the same and identical as to specific intent and purpose as a bill introduced in the other house."</span></li>
  <li><span>Temporarily postponed <b>(TP'd)</b></span><span class="receipt-srcs">postponing consideration of a bill "appearing on an agenda or calendar" — late in session this is often terminal in practice</span></li>
  <li><span>Withdrawn</span><span class="receipt-srcs">"To remove a bill, amendment, or other legislative matter from a committee or from further consideration by the body."</span></li>
  <li><span>Passage</span><span class="receipt-srcs">"Favorable floor action on a bill."</span></li>
  <li><span>Adjournment <em>sine die</em></span><span class="receipt-srcs">"Sine die is the Latin term for 'without day'" — the motion that ends the session, and with it every bill still in play</span></li>
</ul></div>

<h2>How a bill actually ends</h2>
<p>The Legislature's glossaries don't define the phrases you'll see on a dead bill. The <strong>Senate Rules</strong> do — here are the ones that do the killing, with their rule numbers.</p>
<div class="receipts"><ul>
  <li><span>Laid on the table <b>· Rule 2.15</b></span><span class="receipt-srcs">"All bills reported unfavorably shall be laid on the table." An unfavorable committee report doesn't send a bill to the floor to lose — it ends there.</span></li>
  <li><span>Indefinitely postponed <b>· Rule 6.9</b></span><span class="receipt-srcs">The motion that sets a measure aside with no date to return. "Motions to indefinitely postpone shall not be applicable to collateral matters" — it kills the bill itself, not a procedural scrap.</span></li>
  <li><span>Temporarily postponed <b>· Rule 6.11</b></span><span class="receipt-srcs">"The motion to temporarily postpone shall be decided without debate and shall cause a measure to be set aside but retained on the desk." Reversible in theory. Late in session, with the clock running, often not.</span></li>
  <li><span>Withdrawn from further consideration <b>· Rule 6.10</b></span><span class="receipt-srcs">Once a bill is a committee substitute it "may be withdrawn from further consideration only by motion of the introducer and unanimous consent of the Senators present."</span></li>
  <li><span>Withdrawn when a sponsor leaves <b>· Rule 3.12</b></span><span class="receipt-srcs">"Once a Senator is no longer in office, any bill filed by that Senator … shall be deemed withdrawn from further consideration of the Senate unless the bill has a co-introducer who, within seven (7) days, agrees to become the introducer."</span></li>
  <li><span>Adjournment <em>sine die</em> <b>· Joint Rule 8.2</b></span><span class="receipt-srcs">Both houses adjourn by concurrent resolution. Everything unfinished stops where it stands.</span></li>
</ul></div>
<p><strong>"Died in committee" and "died in messages" are not in the rules at all.</strong> They're descriptive labels the clerks apply to a bill's history — plain-English shorthand for a bill that was never agendaed, or that was still moving between the chambers when the clock ran out. Useful to read, but they describe an outcome rather than name a procedure.</p>

<h2>Why most bills end quietly</h2>
<p>Two things in combination do most of the work. A bill is typically referred to <strong>more than one</strong> committee, and it must clear every one of them — so a single chair who never puts it on an agenda can end it without a vote ever being taken. And the sixty-day cap means everything unfinished stops when the gavel falls.</p>
<p>That's the difference between <em>defeated</em> and <em>dead</em>. Very few Florida bills are voted down. Most simply run out of committee agenda, or out of calendar.</p>

